INT. JURY DELIBERATION ROOM - DAY

A bare room, a long table, a window onto a brick wall. Water pitcher, paper cups, a stack of exhibit binders.

COUNSEL OYELARAN, forties, a defense attorney in a charcoal suit, waits with a briefcase beside the table. CLERK MAVIS, sixty, a stout woman with reading glasses on a chain, sets down a tray of sandwiches.

CLERK MAVIS
Counselor, the court says twenty minutes. No more.

OYELARAN
Thank you, Mavis.

CLERK MAVIS
Judge's orders. The juror wants to ask a question and the judge wants counsel present.

OYELARAN
Which juror?

CLERK MAVIS
Number seven. He's been quiet all week.

OYELARAN
Quiet ones worry me.

A knock. Mavis crosses, opens it. A JUROR enters, sixties, in a corduroy blazer, hat in hand. He hesitates at the head of the table.

JUROR 7
May I sit?

OYELARAN
Please.

He sits. Sets his hat on the table. Smooths the brim.

JUROR 7
I don't want to cause trouble.

OYELARAN
Then ask your question.

JUROR 7
The man on trial. Did he really have no alibi?

OYELARAN
That's a matter for the jury, sir.

JUROR 7
I know. But I think I saw him that night.

Mavis's pen stops. Oyelaran sets his briefcase down very slowly.

OYELARAN
Saw him where?

JUROR 7
At the diner on Fifth. Ten-fifteen. Eating pie.

OYELARAN
You're certain?

JUROR 7
Blueberry. He argued with the waitress about the whipped cream.

Oyelaran and Mavis exchange a look. The room is very quiet.

CLERK MAVIS
Sir, you can't share outside evidence with the jury.

JUROR 7
I haven't. I haven't told a soul.

OYELARAN
I need to bring the judge. Please don't speak to anyone.

JUROR 7
My name is Delacroix. Henri Delacroix. If it matters.

OYELARAN
It matters, Mr. Delacroix.

Delacroix nods, picks up his hat, turns it in his hands.

DELACROIX
I thought the truth would be simpler.

OYELARAN
It never is.

Mavis opens the door and steps into the corridor. Oyelaran follows.

OYELARAN (CONT'D)
(low, to Mavis)
Get Judge Hartnell. Now.

CLERK MAVIS
Yes, Counselor.

She leaves at a trot. Oyelaran shuts the door. Delacroix sits alone at the long table, his hat in his lap.
